# Theme elements: element

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mpg = Giggly.Datasets.mpg()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`element()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/element.html), which covers `margin()`,
`margin_part()`, `margin_auto()`, `element_blank()`, `element_rect()`, `element_line()`,
`element_text()`, `element_polygon()`, `element_point()`, `element_geom()` and `rel()` too, in the
order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example
Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also
a list of what remains.

In ggplot2's theme system, the `element_` functions say how the parts of a plot that are not data
are drawn: `element_blank()` draws nothing and takes no space, `element_rect()` draws borders and
backgrounds, `element_line()` lines and `element_text()` text, and `element_geom()` sets the
defaults that layers draw with. `element_polygon()` and `element_point()` are not drawn in standard
plots and serve extensions. `rel()` gives a size relative to the parent element's, and `margin()`,
`margin_part()` and `margin_auto()` give the margins around an element. Giggly sets a plot's
elements with `Giggly.Plot.theme/2`: a line, a rectangle or a text is a keyword list of its
properties, `element_blank()` is `:none`, `rel(1.5)` is `{:rel, 1.5}`, and a margin is one length
for every side or a keyword list of the sides it sets, so `margin()` and `margin_auto()` are written
as lengths. It has no `margin_part()`, `element_geom()`, `element_polygon()` or `element_point()`,
and no `arrow` on a line. A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`,
which returns an SVG document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write
`Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with
`Kino.Image`. The data is `Giggly.Datasets.mpg/0`, ggplot2's `mpg`: fuel economy for 234 cars of 38
popular models from 1999 to 2008, which the setup cell binds to `mpg`, ggplot2's name for it.

## Blank elements

```r
# A standard plot
plot <- ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) + geom_point()

# Turning off theme elements by setting them to blank
plot + theme(
  panel.background = element_blank(),
  axis.text = element_blank()
)
```

```elixir
plot =
  mpg
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :displ, y: :hwy})
  |> Plot.geom_point()

plot
|> Plot.theme(
  panel_background: :none,
  axis_text: :none
)
|> render.()
```

## Styling text

```r
# Text adjustments
plot + theme(
  axis.text = element_text(colour = "red", size = rel(1.5))
)
```

```elixir
plot
|> Plot.theme(axis_text: [colour: "red", size: {:rel, 1.5}])
|> render.()
```

## An axis line with an arrow

```r
# Turning on the axis line with an arrow
plot + theme(
  axis.line = element_line(arrow = arrow())
)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `element_line()`'s `arrow` option, here to end each axis's line in an arrow
made by `grid`'s `arrow()`.

## Backgrounds and margins

```r
plot + theme(
  panel.background = element_rect(fill = "white"),
  plot.margin = margin_auto(2, unit = "cm"),
  plot.background = element_rect(
    fill = "grey90",
    colour = "black",
    linewidth = 1
  )
)
```

`margin_auto(2, unit = "cm")` gives the same 2 cm margin on all four sides, which Giggly writes
as one length, `{2, :cm}`. R's `"grey90"` is a name CSS lacks, so the cell writes its hex,
`"#E5E5E5"`.

```elixir
plot
|> Plot.theme(
  panel_background: [fill: "white"],
  plot_margin: {2, :cm},
  plot_background: [
    fill: "#E5E5E5",
    colour: "black",
    linewidth: 1
  ]
)
|> render.()
```

## Defaults for layers

```r
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(formula = y ~ x, method = "lm") +
  theme(geom = element_geom(
    ink = "red", accent = "black",
    pointsize = 1, linewidth = 2
  ))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_smooth()`'s `formula` option, here `y ~ x`, and `element_geom()`, which
here sets the colours, point size and line width the layers draw with.
